Example of directory file within the clean data folder. 

When you have processed or cleaned a dataset for your scripts,
and they are ready to be visualized, this is where you will save you files. 

This has two advantages.

1 - You can be sure that your raw data is never overwritten. 
That means if you make a mistake, or want to go back and include or remove something, 
you won't have to wade through commits or redownload the raw data.It will stay in its relevant folder.

2 - You can reference files within the project directory that your git is tracking. 
If you saved files outside of the project directory,or used scripts that included your entire directory 
name through your local computer, it would:

  a) Give strangers / scripts on the internet an idea of your computer storage / personal information
  
  b) Not work at the end of the final project when someone is running your code. 
  
As such, when you are saving your code and data, make sure you're using folders within your final project directory!